---
/**
 * Affiliate disclosure.
 *
 * Owner: trust-compliance-officer (docs/agents/ROSTER.md §3). This page publishes the rules that
 * govern commercial links on DelayPilot, and the current state of them: there are none.
 *
 * Every sentence is checked against the repository at the reviewed date rather than against an
 * intention. Verified for this revision: no data/affiliates registry exists; no /go handler is
 * implemented in apps/edge/src/index.ts (the prefix is reserved in wrangler.jsonc and nothing
 * answers on it); AFFILIATE_* ids in .env.example are unset; no anchor anywhere in apps/web/src
 * points at a merchant. If any of that changes, this page is rewritten in the same change — a
 * disclosure that describes yesterday's state is worse than no disclosure.
 *
 * The two disclosure strings are verbatim from DIRECTIVE.md §26 and §20. They are imported from
 * the copy module that owns them rather than retyped, and rendered from a constant so that
 * reformatting this file can never reflow them. They are not paraphrasable: AGENTS.md §7 requires
 * escalation rather than an edit if one reads badly.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { disclaimers } from '../lib/copy/disclaimers.ts'

const title = 'Affiliate disclosure — DelayPilot'
const description =
  'DelayPilot has no affiliate partners and earns nothing from any link on this site. The rules that will govern partner links if that ever changes, stated in advance.'

/**
 * The two disclosure strings are imported, never retyped.
 *
 * apps/web/src/lib/copy/disclaimers.ts (owner: ux-copy-steward) holds them transcribed byte for
 * byte from DIRECTIVE.md §26 (the short form, beside a single standalone link) and §20 (the module
 * form, above the links inside a module), and its test re-reads DIRECTIVE.md and fails on a single
 * changed character. A second copy in this file is precisely the drift that test prevents.
 */
const AFFILIATE_SHORT_DISCLOSURE = disclaimers.affiliate
const AFFILIATE_MODULE_DISCLOSURE = disclaimers.affiliateModule

/**
 * The affiliate categories named in DIRECTIVE.md §20. Every one is disabled: a category is a
 * category, not a partner, and none of them has an agreement, a merchant, or a URL behind it.
 */
const categories = [
  'eSIM data plans',
  'Airport lounges',
  'Nearby hotels',
  'Rental cars',
  'Ground transport',
  'Luggage trackers',
  'Travel accessories',
  'Travel insurance',
  'Replacement travel search',
  'Travel credit cards',
]
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Affiliate disclosure</h1>

    <p>
      <strong>DelayPilot has no affiliate partners.</strong> No link on this site earns DelayPilot a commission,
      a referral fee, a bounty, or any other payment. There is no partner registry, no tracking redirect,
      and no partner module in the product. Nothing on this page is a promise about revenue we already
      take — it is the set of rules we are publishing before there is any money involved, so that the
      rules cannot be written to fit the first payment.
    </p>

    <p class="meta">Reviewed <time datetime="2026-09-12">12 September 2026</time>.</p>

    <h2>The categories, and why every one is switched off</h2>

    <p>
      DelayPilot reserves ten commercial categories. A category is not a partner: it is a slot that
      stays empty until a real, signed agreement with a named merchant exists and the link resolves
      to a real destination. All ten are disabled, which means no link is rendered, no redirect
      resolves, and no identifier is configured.
    </p>

    <ul>
      {
        categories.map((category) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{category}</strong> — disabled.
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p>
      Travel credit cards carry one extra condition beyond an agreement: they are never enabled
      without a completed compliance review, because consumer-credit promotion is regulated
      advertising in most of the places DelayPilot is read.
    </p>

    <h2>The disclosure you will see, before you click</h2>

    <p>
      If a partner link ever appears, the disclosure appears with it — above the links, as visible
      text, readable without hovering, expanding, or scrolling past the link it describes. It is
      never a tooltip, never inside a collapsed section, and never below the fold. Beside a single
      standalone link the text is:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>{AFFILIATE_SHORT_DISCLOSURE}</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>Inside a module that offers more than one partner, the fuller text is used:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>{AFFILIATE_MODULE_DISCLOSURE}</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Both sentences say the same thing twice on purpose. The first half tells you that money may
      change hands. The second half is the commitment that matters more: a commission never changes
      what DelayPilot tells you about your flight, your connection, or the rules that may apply to
      your disruption.
    </p>

    <h2>How partner links are marked</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Every partner link carries <code>rel="sponsored nofollow"</code>, which tells search engines
        the link is commercial and must not pass ranking credit.
      </li>
      <li>
        The merchant is named in the link, and its destination host is visible before you click.
        DelayPilot does not cloak, mask, or disguise where a link goes.
      </li>
      <li>
        Partner links resolve through a redirect that reads only from the partner registry and
        validates the destination against an allowlist. A destination that is not in the registry
        does not redirect. No itinerary detail — no flight number, no route, no date, no traveler
        name, and never a booking reference — is attached to a partner URL or a click record.
      </li>
      <li>
        Today no such redirect is implemented. The path prefix is reserved in the edge Worker's
        configuration and nothing answers on it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where a partner link may never appear</h2>

    <p>
      The ordering rule is absolute, and it applies on a phone exactly as it does on a desktop.
      <strong>
        Useful information and official passenger-rights guidance always come before any commercial
        suggestion.
      </strong> A commercial partner is never presented as the way to obtain a statutory right. If a rule
      may entitle you to a refund, rebooking, or care from the airline, the route to it is the airline
      and the regulator, both linked to their official source — not a checkout.
    </p>

    <p>Specifically, a partner link is never placed:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>inside a passenger-rights card, or next to one;</li>
      <li>inside an action checklist, or next to one;</li>
      <li>
        beside a "Contact airline", "Request refund", or "Save evidence" control, where a mis-click
        costs a traveler time they do not have;
      </li>
      <li>between a warning about a disruption and the action that answers it;</li>
      <li>
        as a travel-insurance offer in a view where a disruption is already under way — insurance
        bought after a delay starts does not cover that delay, and offering it there would be
        selling a traveler something that cannot help them;
      </li>
      <li>as a credit-card promotion inside a crisis action card.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How partners would be ordered</h2>

    <p>
      Partners are ordered by usefulness to the traveler in the situation on screen — proximity,
      coverage, and whether the option can actually be acted on in the time available. DelayPilot
      does not rank purely by commission. If commercial terms ever influence an order, that fact is
      disclosed in the module itself, in the same visible text as the disclosure above. Silent
      pay-for-position is not a thing this product does.
    </p>

    <h2>What DelayPilot earns today</h2>

    <p>
      Nothing. No advertising runs on this site, no affiliate link exists, no placement has been
      sold, and no article, guide, or rights explanation has been paid for, sponsored, or reviewed
      by a commercial partner. When that changes, it changes here first: this page is rewritten in
      the same change that enables a partner, naming the category and the merchant, and the change
      is recorded with the date it took effect.
    </p>

    <h2>Related policies</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/advertising-policy/">Advertising policy</a> — where an ad may and may not run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial policy</a> — how guidance is sourced and reviewed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/terms/">Terms of use</a> — what DelayPilot is, and what it never does for you.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
